# {{ name | kebab_case }}

An S3-compatible object store for uploads, generated by the `storage` Moon
template. It runs [SeaweedFS](https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs) in one
container and creates the bucket `{{ bucket }}`.

```bash
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:up      # start it and create the bucket
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:down    # stop it, keeping the data
```

The S3 API listens on `127.0.0.1:{{ port }}` (`PORT` in `.env.example`; Moon checks
it against the other services' ports). Its data is in a Docker volume named after
this repository folder; `docker compose down -v` here deletes it.

The API that handles uploads reads these from its `.env`; the example file has
the local ones:

| Setting | Local value |
| --- | --- |
| `S3_ENDPOINT` | `http://localhost:{{ port }}` |
| `S3_BUCKET` | `{{ bucket }}` |
| `S3_ACCESS_KEY` | `local-access-key` (from `s3.json`) |
| `S3_SECRET_KEY` | `local-secret-key` |

A browser uploads to a presigned URL, so the web apps' origins are listed in
`S3_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`.

## Production

Use a managed S3-compatible service, with a bucket that is not public and keys
that only the upload API holds. The API signs URLs for whatever `S3_ENDPOINT`
says. Set the bucket's CORS rule to the origins of your web apps and the methods
`PUT` and `GET`. The keys in `s3.json` are for local development only.
